%S A399828 1,2,2,3,2,4,2,4,3,4,2,6,2,4,4,4,2,6,2,6,4,4,2,8,3,4,4,6,2,8,2,6,4,4,
%T A399828 4,9,2,4,4,8,2,8,2,6,6,4,2,8,3,6,4,6,2,8,4,8,4,4,2,12,2,4,6,6,4,8,2,6,
%U A399828 4,8,2,12,2,4,6,6,4,8,2,8,4,4,2,12,4,4,4,8,2,12
%N A399828 The number of divisors d of n such that gcud(d, n/d) is squarefree, where gcud is the greatest common unitary divisor.
%C A399828 First differs from A252505, A365173, A365680, A366991, and A390555 at n = 32, and from A399831 at n = 64.
%C A399828 The sum of these divisors is A399834(n).
%H A399828 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A399828/b399828.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399828 Vaclav Kotesovec, <a href="/A399828/a399828.jpg">Graph - the asymptotic ratio (10000 terms)</a>
%F A399828 Multiplicative with a(p^e) = e + 1 for e <= 2, and a(p^e) = e + e mod 2 for e >= 3.
%F A399828 Dirichlet g.f.: zeta(s) * zeta(2*s) * Product_{p prime} (1 + 1/p^s - 1/p^(4*s) + 1/p^(5*s)).
%F A399828 From _Vaclav Kotesovec_, Sep 24 2026: (Start)
%F A399828 Let f(s) = Product_{p prime} 1 - (p^s - 1) / ((p^s + 1) * p^(4*s)).
%F A399828 Dirichlet g.f.: zeta(s)^2 * f(s).
%F A399828 Sum_{k=1..n} a(k) ~ f(1) * n * (log(n) + 2*gamma - 1 + f'(1)/f(1)), where
%F A399828 f(1) = Product_{p prime} 1 - (p-1) / ((p+1) * p^4) = A393891 = 0.9716720018400523904343598356670186989195235143102572014260177765789918...,
%F A399828 f'(1) = f(1) * Sum_{p prime} 2*(2*p^2-p-2) * log(p) / ((p+1) * (p^5+p^4-p+1)) = f(1) * 0.0710303451985375815625408260793827448370713199680540232653140469823646...
%F A399828 and gamma is the Euler-Mascheroni constant A001620. (End)
%t A399828 f[p_, e_] := If[e == 2, 3, e + Mod[e, 2]]; a[1] = 1; a[n_] := Times @@ f @@@ FactorInteger[n]; Array[a, 100]
%o A399828 (PARI) a(n) = vecprod(apply(x -> if(x < 3, x+1, x + x%2), factor(n)[, 2]));
%Y A399828 Cf. A005117, A077610, A165430, A225174, A384244.
%Y A399828 Cf. A252505 (analogous with gcd), A399834.
%Y A399828 Cf. A365173, A365680, A366991, A390555.
%Y A399828 Cf. A393891.
%Y A399828 The number of divisors d of n such that gcud(d, n/d) is: A286324 (1), this sequence (squarefree), A399829 (powerful), A399830 (square), A399831 (exponentially odd), A399832 (odd), A399833 (power of 2).
%K A399828 nonn,mult,easy,new
%O A399828 1,2
%A A399828 _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 13 2026
